<Layout
title="Making a website — Mike Trounce"
description="A few different ways I've built websites for clients."
>
<div class="options-page">
<a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>

<h1>Making a website</h1>
<p class="intro">
A few different routes I've taken for clients, depending on budget, how much the site needs to do, and how often it'll change.
</p>
<p class="tech-flag">These are plain-language summaries — each one links to a full case study, where the technical detail is broken out separately.</p>

<section class="project">
<h2>Saving The Greensman £100+ a year</h2>
<p class="project-meta">Quick turnaround &middot; Elstead, Surrey &middot; <a href="/case-study-1">Case study 1</a></p>
<p>
<a href="https://www.thegreensmanelstead.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thegreensmanelstead.com</a> is Freddy Trounce's site for his garden maintenance business. It didn't need a redesign — it needed cheaper hosting. The site was already on Wix's Light plan (£108/year) plus a domain (£17/year), set up by the original developer, likely as much in exchange for gardening work as for cash. Moving it to a self-hosted setup took very little time and will save Freddy over £100 a year, starting next year.
</p>
<p>
While I was in there, I also swapped the site's handful of hand-picked testimonials for an embedded feed of Freddy's Google Business reviews. It was a quick change, but it means the site now updates itself — new reviews appear automatically as clients leave them on Google, without Freddy (or me) needing to touch the code.
</p>
<p>
The website was one part of a longer job for Freddy — the flyer, the brand, and whatever else turned up. <a href="/case-study-1">Read the full case study &rarr;</a>
</p>
</section>

<section class="project">
<h2>This website</h2>
<p class="project-meta">Personal site · <a href="/case-study-2">Case study 2</a></p>
<p>
miketrounce.com is built the same way: figure out the one job the site actually needs to do, then build only that. No blog, no CMS, nothing added because a website "should" have it.
</p>
<p>
It's a fast, simple site to run — and, if it ever needs more than that, the foundation underneath it can grow to meet it. <a href="/case-study-2">Read the full case study →</a>
</p>
</section>

